<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Consolation of Philosophy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 524</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as De consolatione philosophiae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Consolation of Philosophy is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> work by the Roman statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boethius">Boethius</a>, written around 524 while he awaited execution after falling from favour with the Ostrogothic king Theoderic. In alternating prose and verse, Lady Philosophy consoles the prisoner by showing that true happiness lies in God rather than fortune. It was one of the most widely read books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and was translated into Old English in the circle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-consolation-of-philosophy.jpg" alt="The Consolation of Philosophy" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boethius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boethius,_Consolatio_philosophiae_(French).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
